.TH LD 1 "" "New Human Shell"
.SH NAME
LD \- load a prose program and dispatch it to a reasoning runner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B LD
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I program
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B LD
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B LD
accepts a readable regular file at the specified path, relative to the
working directory when appropriate. If a name contains no slash and does
not name a regular file in the working directory,
.B LD
uses
.B command \-v
to try to find it on
.BR PATH .
The resulting path must name a readable regular file. A prose program
supplies UTF-8 instructions to the runner. To invoke a program directly,
give it execute permission and start it with
.BR "#!/usr/bin/env LD" .
.B LD
itself only requires read permission on the program file.
.PP
.B LD
sets
.B NSH_PROGRAM
to the resolved file and replaces itself with the selected runner.
The runner receives a double dash followed by the remaining request
arguments and inherits standard input from the loader. The loader consumes
an optional double dash immediately after the program name. Other
arguments after the program name, including option-like arguments, are
passed through unchanged for the runner to interpret.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage to standard output and exit successfully when used as the
first argument.
.TP
.B \-\-
Before the program name, end option handling so a name resembling an
option can be used.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LD_RUNNER
Runner to execute. If unset or empty, defaults to
.BR lordhavemercy .
.TP
.B PATH
Search path for program names and runners. It also lets
.B env
find
.B LD
when a program is invoked via its shebang.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B LD
returns 2 if the program argument is missing or the resolved path does not
name a readable regular file. If the runner cannot be found or executed, the
shell reports the error. After a successful handoff, the runner supplies
the exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
LD @ 'explain this command'
LD_RUNNER=lord LD './my program' -- 'summarize the input' < input.txt
LD @ -- -c
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR @ (1),
.BR lord (1)
